      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Empirical evidence on the direct impact of facial recognition on crime reduction within public transport remains complex and debated. Proponents often cite case studies showing quicker identification of wanted individuals, but critics point to false positive rates and system inaccuracies in dense environments. While proponents claim efficiency gains in boarding gates, real-world data supporting significant, measurable throughput improvements versus overall security gains is often lacking or proprietary. Overall, the claims often outpace demonstrable, scalable success in practice.",
